{
  "dom_id": "step-q101840",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find the intersection of intervals $[1,8)\\cap (4,8].$ Express the result as an inequality.",
  "worked_solution": "We start by sketching the two intervals $[1,8)$ and $(4,8]$ separately:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/63a236a899eff365f7964a182690470011cbee2f393586fb3a673e5332b7346e.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/9b2db1951a30f71c8635e0daec81ae6931e28c2c44489952c65e10d4c95ea549.png)\n\nThe intersection $[1,8)\\cap (4,8]$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/f59e4645230b48b89fb445fd3cb586516361848418e2d287e6d208c97d9dcc00.png)\n\nThus, $[1,8)\\cap (4,8]=(4,8).$ This corresponds to the inequality $4<x<8.$",
